From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:20:28 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 190/194] hjviewer: review fixes for the connection window's
 pipe, one name per Windows session

What a user would see: with the two previous commits (the pipe's protection
and the wait for a refused window), a connection window left open in
another Windows session kept \\.\pipe\HJViewer\query_cm. The window stays
open after a file transfer or an unanswered chat (connection.rs on_close
sends Data::Disconnected, ui_cm_interface keeps the connection with
close=false, flutter/lib/models/server_model.dart then never reaches its
6 s auto-close). After fast user switching or a remote desktop disconnect
to another account, every incoming connection to that account's session
was refused by the --server and ended 12 s later with "connection manager
error", and so were its clipboard and privacy-mode sends, until the first
account came back and closed the window. 1.9.3 accepted these connections
(in the wrong user's window).

Why: Windows pipe names are one namespace for every session, so "_cm" was a
single name for the whole PC, while the --server now trusts only its own
session's window (src/ipc/windows.rs ensure_server).

What changes: the pipe is named per session on Windows, "_cm_s<session id>"
(src/ipc.rs cm_postfix). Every process that uses it builds the name from
its own session: the window (ui_cm_interface start_ipc), the --server that
starts the window in its own session (connection.rs start_ipc ->
run_as_user -> run_exe_in_cur_session), its clipboard
(clipboard_service.rs) and privacy mode (privacy_mode.rs), the main window
(ui_interface send_to_cm) and the e2e stand-in. A window left open in
another session now holds only that session's name; the --server of this
session starts its own window as usual. is_cm_postfix (used by
is_windows_protected_ipc, listener_security_attributes and
expected_server_user) takes "_cm" and "_cm_s<digits>", so every session's
pipe keeps the 1.9.4 protection. If the session id cannot be read the name
falls back to "_cm", on both sides alike.

Mixed versions: update_me and the installer end every HJViewer.exe in every
session before the new service starts, so no 1.9.3 window (on "_cm") is
left to meet a 1.9.4 --server; if one were, the --server finds its own name
missing and starts a 1.9.4 window, which works.

Left alone: Linux and macOS keep "_cm" (their socket is in a directory per
user). Another account can still take this session's name while no window
is open (a denial of service, no data); the wait for a refused pipe in
start_ipc stays for that case and for another copy of the app.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
